   "lectureId": "BRGSA-M01-L01",
   "title": "Lean Validation",
   "text": "This topic adds the layer that comes before all of that, before even the campaign runs, before even your product is built, before even that pricing page exists on your business. There is a simpler, more fundamental question that most teams never formally ask, and that is, does anyone actually want or does anyone will actually pay what we are planning to build? Will real people take a real action in response to a real offer around a product or our business idea? Lean validation is the discipline of answering that question as quickly and as cheaply as possible. Now, you'll still be measuring. It's just that you're measuring much earlier at a stage where the answer to that question can still change what you're building in your business, much before you actually start running marketing campaigns for your product or business idea. Now, here is the definition of lean validation. You can simply write it down. Lean validation is testing whether the real demand exists before you even invest in building your product or business idea. You might have seen that we start building the, \"Hey, this sounds to be a cool startup idea. Let's start building.\" And then suddenly after 1 or 2 years, companies or teams find out we are not getting any leads or we're not getting any sales because maybe the people or the actual target customers didn't even exist. No one wants to buy that idea at this point of time."
