   "title": "Early stage Versus Scale stage Growth",
   "text": "So how do you make that transition is something, because both the systems will be running at the same time when you're making that transition, and this should typically take 12 to 18 months because you just cannot flip a switch that, \"Hey, now we are not a growth stage company anymore. Tomorrow we build the system and we start with the system only.\" So when this transition happens, there is a risk of few things breaking here and there and few of the 4 specific things. Let's talk about 4 specific things which could break. The first could be the founder context doesn't scale here. Because in a single stage or a early stage company, it is just the founder who is actually doing most of the work or the growth leader is doing more, most of the work. They have the context. They know each and everything. They know about the customers. They know about the process. When you're going into scale stage, then that context cannot scale on itself. There has to be definite systems, dashboards, and reports which states all the intelligence about the whole process instead of depending on 1 or 2 people. Now let's say you are having a 1000 customers, and if you're still dependent on few people, then it becomes a bottleneck because then the decision will be delayed since only few people have the context. The second risk is the ad hoc experiments will become chaos because initially what was happening is one person didn't need a system."
