  {
   "citation": "BRGSA-M05-L06#01",
   "lectureId": "BRGSA-M05-L06",
   "title": "Activation Metric Design",
   "text": "This topic is about activation metric design. Specifically, what number you put on the dashboard to measure whether your activation system is working in the right direction or not. So far, we learned about the aha moment, the precise event when a user first experiences your product's promised value. Then we audited the road, the friction between sign-up and the destination. We removed what didn't earn its place to reach to the aha moment. This topic answers what do you measure in doing all of this particular process? Because the wrong activation metric might drive wrong work for the years, and the teams might optimize what is actually wrong. Because if they're measuring the wrong thing, they will end up optimizing the wrong thing as well. By the end of this topic, you will be able to construct an activation metric and understand what exactly it is. Five things that we will focus here. First, how to distinguish or learn to distinguish an activation event from an activation metric. Second, construct a well-formed metric using the four components of event, time window, cohort, and the denominator. And third, we'll decompose some famous example metrics into the four components. Fourth, we will choose a defensible time window and denominator for your chosen brand. And the fifth thing, we will recognize the vanity metrics, vague metrics, and the biased proxy metrics. Now, we already know that aha event is what exactly a user achieves, which is the perceived value."
  }
