   "text": "team? Because most of the times teams treat that, \"Hey, I've given you the lead. I have, like, my job is done.\" So, the handoffs are not one way. Without the right to reject, the receiving team will become that, say, marketing team will say that they gave, like, 100 MQs. SDR might say, \"I gave 75 SQLs,\" even though they were actually not right. So, it has to be 2-way handoff. So, now look at the matrix on the screen. Marketing to SDR, the criteria should be MQS The MQL definitions should be met. The time SLA could be, let's say, 24 hours. The context that would be shared from marketing to SDR, the source, the behavior, the ICP score, and the acceptance could be that SDR can return if these criteria are not actually met. Suppose if that handoff is happening from SDR to AE or pre-sales to sales team, the criteria would be SQL. The SQL definitions should be met. The context would be, let's say, discovery notes, budget, decisionmaker, timeline, et cetera, that they will exactly hand it off. From AE to CSM, that, that means from the sales team to the customer success team, the criteria would be, of course, contract signed that has become a paid customer. The time SLA would be the kickoff should be scheduled within 72 hours because the customer has paid, it's become a customer. The customer success should actually kick off like schedule the kickoff call with your customer within 72 hours. Again, this depends on the company to company."
