   "text": "you know that the activation is where the leak actually is and it is a primary constraint. And that is the central job of ICE framework to prioritize your primary constraint wherever that constraint stays, activation or acquisition or retention or wherever it is. Now, let's look at the 4 ways that teams generally break the ICE framework execution in the real corporate world. First is confidence inflation. Everyone scores their own idea at a 9. The fix for this rule is I gave you earlier. If you cannot name a piece of evidence in one sentence, your confidence caps at maximum 5. The second is scoring in isolation. Now, solo scoring drifts because everyone has a slightly different sense of what a 7 means versus an 8. So, the fix is to score the first 3 ideas as a team openly calibrating the language until a 7 means roughly the same thing to everyone in the room. Third is the ease for CEO or maybe let's say, ease for head of marketing versus the ease for team. Now, the leaders might think that, \"Hey, let us do this. This is quick to implement,\" but maybe the team might not have that capability or maybe they might not have the bandwidth. So, the fix is that you score from the perspective of the team who are going to execute the work, perform the measurement and the analysis and then also read the signals. The fourth problem that comes in is the static scores. Now, we know that ICE scores are not permanent."
