   "title": "Decision Rules",
   "text": "time or a certain sample size. Write each rule the way you would write a contract. Specific numbers, specific units, and no directional language. No approximately. The numbers are anchored to your brand's actual baseline metrics from the brand bible that you already have for both Clairo and Zoko. You will use this as the seed of tab three of your capstone revenue and experiment model. The decision scorecard tab. You now have a rule that can decide what an experiment outcome means. But every rule rests on one assumption that we have quietly accepted throughout this topic, that the metric that we have chosen is actually the right metric. But suppose if you don't know that it is the right metric or not, if you're measuring the wrong number, the rule decides the wrong thing, and that is worse than no rule at all. And that is the next topic. Avoiding vanity metrics or the wrong metrics or the numbers. Which numbers actually move the business? Which numbers just feel like they do, but they do not? And how to tell the difference before you put a metric in a decision rule? That's all for this particular topic. See you in the next one."
