   "text": "Suppose, they would have a single 1,500 rupee per seat plan. No pro plan, no team plan, no enterprise plan. Now I open the 12-month projection tab. Look at the month 12 cell, tiered structure projects around 42 lakh of MRR, or which we also say as monthly recurring revenue. Wherein the flat rate structure projects only 32 lakh MRR. You can sclearly see that there's a 10 lakh per month gap by month 12 from the same customer base just by changing the pricing structure. And here is the sensitivity test. Let's say if I zero out the upgrade rate in this flat rate scenario, that gap closes because the flat rate has no structural upgrade path. Whatever expansion happens has to come from sales effort and not from the structure. So, first important call-out. The current per seat tiered structure is producing an 18% Pro to Team upgrade rate. That upgrade rate is not a sales achievement, rather it is a pricing structural consequence. The Team plan is priced to make that upgrade rational so that users, like, choose an upgrade which with no guilt. The second important call-out is if you flatten the Clairos pricing to a single 1,500 rupee plan, it would drop the projected MRR by roughly around 23%. So, the flat rate captures the same average revenue per account, but it removes the expansion path. And we have already seen that the reason is mathematical and not just strategic. If we talk about tiered per seat structure, it compounds, wherein flat rate structure does not compound."
