   "title": "Introduction to Cohort Analysis",
   "text": "Now let's look at from the same number from the cohort view. On day 7, their retention is 48%. That mean more than half of all users who signed up are gone within the first 7 days. Month one retention is 31%. That means less than a third are actually present after 30 days who have signed up to their platform. Month 3, let's say it is 22%. And if you go on real kind real companies or real products, the numbers nearly looks like the same. If there's a very good product, those numbers could be better. On an average, generally numbers looks the same for the companies. So, let's get back to the Clairo example. So, by the time a user has been signed up for 3 months, 78% of them have already left. So, that 8,400 total is actually not 8,400 active users. It's just a 22% of them by month 3. So, only a fraction of your people who are actually signing up are using the product for Clairo. Now, for a growth decision, this changes everything. It's not 8,400, it's 20% of them. If you looked at aggregate numbers and you said, \"Hey, let us double the acquisition. Let's spend more money. Let's bring more people,\" you will drive more users into a product which is already losing 78% of them within the first 90 days itself. And why are we doing this? So, in such a case, what will happen? Will you not increase the acquisition? Yes, we would. But a good growth team, what they would do, \"Hey, let's not look at this whole 8,400 people."
