   "title": "Early LTV Logic Estimating Lifetime Value Before you have the Data",
   "text": "Misconception 3, that LTV is one fixed number for the whole product, which is actually not true, and we have already seen that from few examples from the previous topic itself. That LTV can vary by the customer segment, and we saw that the Zoko subscribers have 4.5 times the LTV of the people who were just one-time buyers. Now, this is the same company, the product is same, but the behaviors are different of 2 different kind of customer segments. Now, let's talk about what is the formula for LTV. So, LTV equals to ARPU multiplied by average customer lifespan, multiplied by gross margin percentage. 3 inputs I spoke to you. I will walk through each one by one. So, let's talk about first input, which is ARPU or A-R-P-U. That is average revenue per user or average revenue per account. Depends on how the companies or the teams are actually calculating in their own decision frameworks. So, for example, for Clairo, their total MRR or the monthly recurring revenue stands, let's say, at 14.2 lakhs, which gets divided by 312 paying accounts, which might equals to somewhere, let's say, 4,551 rupees per account per month. That is the actual collected average revenue per user. Here in this particular case, it is per company. It could be per individual user, depends whether we are a B2B or a B2C company. Similarly, for Zoko subscribers, their AOV is 1,380 per month."
