   "text": "Most products do not fail because users hate them. They fail because users just forget about them and that is the topic. How do products become routines that come back without you chasing the user? Everything else in this module sits on top of this one mechanic, the habit loop, life cycle, retention, referral, churn. The habit loop is underneath all of them. You get this right, and the rest automatically gets easier. Now, five outcomes from this topic. First, you will diagnose retention as a habit problem and not just a communication problem. Second, you will know the 4 phases of the hook model, the trigger, action, variable, reward, investment. Third, the difference between an external trigger and an internal trigger. The fourth point, you will design a habit loop for both of our brands, Clairo and Zoko. And the fifth point, when a loop breaks, you should be able to point at the specific phase that actually failed. The thread or the mantra that connects all five is that the retention is engineered and not just earned randomly. You might already know about email automation, life cycle drip, and maybe customer journey mapping as well. You might also know how to run an A/B test, let's say, on a CTA button or on something else on a landing page, and you might also know what an activation rate is by now. What we are doing here is the layer underneath all of that. Because a life cycle email is a push, and you're pushing the user to come back."
