   "lectureId": "BRGSA-M07-L01",
   "title": "Pricing Models",
   "text": "The same LTV or 12 month LTV for the subscription customer is around 14,400, which is, let's say, 4.5 times more. So, where does that gap actually come from? And that is from the subscription continuation, because Zoko subscription month 2 or the continuation rate is 74%, month 6 continuation is 48%, which means a customer who signs up for the subscription is likely to keep paying for at least 6 months, while a one time customer has to choose to come back every single time. So, the first repurchase rate for one time buyers is, let's say, 22% in month one, 22% versus 74%, that is the structural retention gap between these 2 pricing models. So, we can see the products are same, the brand is same, but 2 structural pricing choices is what creates this huge difference. The customer who chose subscription is worth 4 and a half times more over a year. So, the structure is doing the work and not the product here, or maybe not the marketing. It's just the structure that is helping you to increase that revenue 4.5 times for the same brands, the same product and nearly from the same users. Now, Zoko also has a fourth structural lever. So, let's notice this, and that is, let's say, called"
