   "text": "invested your time, money and effort because of a type II error where you actually got a false negative, you will not invest that time in the right direction. So, the losses in terms of time, cost and effort is not here, but then you missed an opportunity because you discard a better variant and continue with the worse baseline. So, over the period of time, the benefit that you could have actually achieved, you will not be able to achieve because you've got a false negative due to this type II error. Now, unlike type I error, this one is visi, invisible, you should say. Because type I error is visible, after a certain point of time you will feel that, \"hey, we have invested so much and no results are coming, which we actually thought in the first place.\" In type II error, you will never be able to, like, view that or see that in the real action because you simply missed that opportunity. So, but what you have missed, it will actually compound and you will never be able to see that. So, let's take 2 type of examples. For Clairo, suppose the team test a new onboarding flow. They required, suppose, 1,400 people per variant. Now, let's say, they didn't have budget, so they cut the window and they only did the test with 310 users. And the test shows no significant result. So, the team abandons the new onboarding flow, which actually should have not been done because they had insufficient data, so they were not able to detect it."
