 "ibm_vaatsalya_short_answer": [
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L04#03",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L04",
   "title": "Vaatsalya Hospital Case Study",
   "text": "inclusive business in the healthcare sector. You can also look at some of the videos which are available on Vaatsalya Hospital on YouTube. Now, what was the primary model of Vaatsalya? Vaatsalya Hospital wanted to establish low cost hospitals in semi-urban India to provide basic healthcare services. I would now remind you once again of the inverted pyramid which we talked about in the healthcare sector. If you remember, we said that the biggest demand supply mismatch is happening in the primary sector and in rural India. This is exactly the problem that Vaatsalya Healthcare tried to address. They realized that there are lot of doctors in urban India and there are lot of hospitals provided tertiary care, but there is a huge demand of primary and secondary healthcare in the rural India, and can we do something about it to meet those demands? Because if you do not meet the demand at that point in rural India, then the poor people will have to incur a lot of costs, lot of expenses to get proper treatment from the cities where they need to come. They also realized that only one hospital will not be enough. Can we establish a chain of hospitals starting in Karnataka and then maybe spreading all across India? If you have to set up these kind of hospitals, one of the most obvious things to do is to cut down your cost. And hence, they also built in that mechanism saying that, \"How can I deliver healthcare in a very low frills, low price model?"
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L04#04",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L04",
   "title": "Vaatsalya Hospital Case Study",
   "text": "I will have to deliver quality, but at the same time I will also have to economize on the cost.\" So, this is the very broad framework by which Vaatsalya started to set up their hospitals. We would want to understand by reading and analyzing the case that, what was the need that these hospitals were addressing? Why were these needs not satisfied by the markets or commercial enterprises? And why does Vaatsalya need to be an inclusive business model? Why does Va-Vatsala need to be a social enterprise? If you think of each of these questions, you will realize that when it comes to semi-urban or rural location, markets don't operate there very well because of scarcity, which we already discussed. There is dearth of doctors, there is less availability of associated services and as a result, you don't get proper healthcare in semi-urban or rural location. And that's the reason why Vaatsalya had to be a social enterprise. If it wanted to maximize its profitability, the choice would have been to open one more big hospital in urban India. You go to any of these cities, despite the presence of so many hospitals, you will find all of these hospitals full of patients, and patients are waiting in queues, which means the demand is almost unending. So, if you want to be profitable, the best way to do it is to open another hospital in urban India, maybe in a popular city. But that's not what the founders of Vaatsalya wanted to do."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L04#05",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L04",
   "title": "Vaatsalya Hospital Case Study",
   "text": "They realized that it is expensive for the poor people to travel from rural India or semi-urban India to get good quality treatment and hence they said, \"Can we set up low cost, no-frills chain of hospitals in rural India itself?\" As we look at the case, we would want to understand what are the key drivers which enabled this business model. What were the key problems that they would face if they wanted to set up a set of low-cost hospitals? And how did the founders decide to overcome those problems? How could they have established a sustainable business, which means the business will have to be profitable and at the same time address the needs of the poor? That, by the way, was our definition of inclusive business models. We also had the third question, that if we have a successful model, can we scale it up? So, we will come and look at that question saying that if Vaatsalya is able to set up about 5 or 6 hospitals, get their model right, get the confidence that it is possible for you to deliver healthcare to the poor at a low price, can they make those numbers multiply maybe 10 times, 20 times because the demand is so huge. And finally, we will also compare Vaatsalya's model with some of the other models which are also inclusive in nature. Inclusive healthcare delivered by Narayana Health and by Arvind Eye Hospital."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L04#06",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L04",
   "title": "Vaatsalya Hospital Case Study",
   "text": "When we look at all of these 3 models, Narayana Health, Arvind Eye Care and Vaatsalya Hospital and compare them, we will get some insights about what is possible and what is not possible as far as inclusive healthcare is concerned."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L04#02",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L04",
   "title": "Vaatsalya Hospital Case Study",
   "text": "They don't happen equally every year. And hence for the farmers to have continuous income for them to grow crops, irrigation is a very important factor. We will talk about this once again when we are looking at the rural supply chain. But do keep this in mind that these were found to be the important factors which enabled families to come out of poverty. If you remember the discussion that we had between C.K. Prahalad and Aneel Karnani, Professor Karnani was also talking about the same thing, that if you want to help the poor, the best way to help them is to provide with employment, not really to sell them some fancy products and services which C.K. Prahalad was arguing. This also builds on what Professor Karnani was talking about. We now get into the discussion of Vaatsalya Hospitals. The case study has already been shared with you. Please go through the case study to understand how Vatsalya's founders were able to build an"
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L04#01",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L04",
   "title": "Vaatsalya Hospital Case Study",
   "text": "Since we have seen that what are those reasons which make families become poor, you might also be intrigued to find out what are some of those reasons which made people come out of poverty, which is the good part of the story. And because Professor Krishna was looking at all of this data, he and his teams also came up with the reason which enable families or which typically enable poor families to come out of poverty. And the table gives you this result. You will find that the biggest reason or the best factor by which people are able to come out of poverty is income diversification, which means that maybe you are growing one particular crop, now you are able to grow multiple crops. Or maybe instead apart from farming, you also do a little bit of animal rearing, fishing or you go and take up another job. What income diversification helps is to spread your risk. If something happens to one source of income and if all your eggs are in that basket, then you really become poor. Whereas if your income is diversified, you have been doing multiple activities. In that case, you can deal with those shocks that might happen from time to time. Employment, you will find is another big reason, whether it is with government or whether it is with private sector. You will also find assistance from the government or nonprofits, as well as irrigation. Irrigation, as you will see that most of the names there are from India, which is typically dependent on rain, but rains are moody."
  }
 ],
