   "citation": "IBM-M02-L08#05",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L08",
   "title": "Aravind Eye Hospital",
   "text": "If you think of the process now, you will clearly understand that productivity will almost go up three times now. The surgeons were doing more operations and more and more patients could be treated by the same set of surgeons. The numbers tell it all. The average ophthalmologist conducts anything between 1200 to 2400 surgeries a year compared to national averages of only about 220 to 250. The operation tables, as I told you, have 2 or 3. The operation theaters have 2 or 3 tables, each of which was happening in almost parallel so, that the patients are always ready for the surgeon to do the surgery. Typically, there is a large team of surgeons and several nurses and paramedics who operate multiple cases as high as 30 in an hour. Now, when you do all this, doctors and staff are also expected to put in very long hours because they are conducting so, many surgeries, they are dealing with so, many patients But once you do that, the efficiency of the system increases dramatically and Aravind was able to do almost three or four times more surgery than otherwise it would have happened. But keep in mind that to keep this assembly line of patients, you have to have a large inflow of patients. If there isn't a large inflow of patients, the surgeon will not have enough patients to conduct the surgery."
